    content: `
      <p>The starting answer for any Chicago high-rise condominium board: life-safety requirements are established by the Chicago Municipal Code and administered by City departments, and what applies to your building depends on its height, age, construction type, and existing systems. The board's first job is to obtain a <strong>written, building-specific statement of applicable requirements</strong> from a qualified fire-protection engineer or life-safety consultant, confirmed with the City where anything is unclear. Do not rely on assumptions, on what a neighboring building did, or on institutional memory from a prior board.</p>

      <p>The second job is treating compliance as an ongoing program. Systems require inspection, testing, and maintenance on defined cycles; records must be retained and producible; residents need current information; and the board must document its decisions. Buildings rarely fail because the board refused to act -- they fail because responsibility drifted between boards and vendors and no one held the calendar.</p>

      <h2>Inventory the Systems You Actually Have</h2>
      <p>Document each life-safety system in the building with its make, age, service history, and monitoring arrangement. Depending on the building this may include fire alarm and detection, voice communication, sprinklers and standpipes, fire pumps, smoke control and stair pressurization, emergency and standby power, emergency lighting and exit signage, elevator recall and firefighter service, and fire-rated doors and assemblies. An accurate inventory is the foundation for every subsequent decision.</p>

      <h3>Maintain a Testing and Inspection Calendar</h3>
      <p>Each system has its own required inspection, testing, and maintenance frequency. Consolidate them into one calendar with owners, vendors, and due dates. Confirm the applicable frequencies with your consultant and the City rather than adopting generic intervals.</p>

      <h2>Records That Withstand Review</h2>
      <p>Retain inspection and test reports, deficiency notices and proof of correction, permits, monitoring agreements, vendor certifications and insurance, and board minutes reflecting life-safety decisions. These records also matter to insurance underwriters, lenders reviewing the project, and prospective buyers. Under the Illinois Condominium Property Act, owners have rights to access specified association records, and your declaration may add requirements, so ask association counsel how these materials should be organized and produced.</p>

      <h2>Resident Communication and Emergency Planning</h2>
      <p>A high-rise plan only works if residents know it. Boards should maintain and periodically distribute clear guidance on evacuation versus shelter-in-place instructions specific to the building's design, stairwell locations and access, elevator use during emergencies, and how alarms are annunciated. Ask your consultant to confirm the correct guidance for your building's systems -- generic advice can be actively harmful in the wrong building type. Maintain a voluntary list of residents who may need assistance, handled with appropriate privacy care under counsel's guidance.</p>

      <h2>Board Life-Safety Checklist</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Obtain a written, building-specific statement of applicable City requirements from a qualified consultant.</li>
        <li>Build and maintain a complete life-safety system inventory.</li>
        <li>Consolidate all inspection, testing, and maintenance cycles into a single calendar.</li>
        <li>Contract with licensed, insured fire-protection vendors and verify certifications annually.</li>
        <li>Track every deficiency to written closure with proof of correction.</li>
        <li>Retain all reports, permits, and correspondence in an organized, producible file.</li>
        <li>Fund life-safety upgrades in the capital plan rather than deferring them.</li>
        <li>Distribute building-specific emergency guidance to residents at least annually.</li>
        <li>Coordinate with the fire department where appropriate for familiarization.</li>
        <li>Record life-safety decisions and expenditures in board minutes.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Program Management Support</h2>
      <p>Life-safety programs demand calendars, vendor oversight, and records discipline. Our <a href="/services/maintenance-coordination">maintenance coordination</a>, <a href="/services/financial-management">financial management</a>, and <a href="/services/board-support">board support</a> services help <a href="/services/condominium-management">condominium boards</a> throughout <a href="/property-management-chicago">Chicago</a> keep compliance programs running across board turnover.</p>

      <p><em>This article is educational information for association boards, not legal or engineering advice. Confirm current City of Chicago requirements with the applicable departments and retain a qualified fire-protection professional and association counsel for your building.</em></p>

      <p><a href="/contact">Schedule a consultation</a> to review your building's life-safety program.</p>
    `,
